James inquired about manipulating arrays in Julia, specifically focusing on dropping rows or columns from a 2D array, converting boolean values to symbols, and determining symbols based on the maximum values among three arrays. For dropping a row or column, I provided methods using slicing and `setdiff`. To convert an array of booleans to symbols (`:buy` and `:hold`), I suggested both comprehension and mapping methods. Finally, I explained how to generate a vector of symbols (`:aa`, `:bb`, `:cc`) by comparing three arrays element-wise, using both comprehension and a custom function with `map`. Each solution was complemented with example code and expected output.
